PULSEBI – DATASET TRAINING DOCUMENTATION

DOCUMENT STATUS

This document describes a proposed machine-learning extension for
PulseBI. It is planning/documentation only. It does NOT mean that a
model has already been trained, evaluated, or integrated into the
application.

1. PROJECT OVERVIEW

PulseBI is a Flask-based Business Intelligence and Sales Analysis
application. It processes sales data and presents analytical summaries,
visualizations, business insights, and downloadable reports.

2. PROPOSED MACHINE-LEARNING EXTENSION

Objective: Use historical sales records to train a regression model that
estimates a selected target, such as Profit or Revenue.

Suggested algorithm: Random Forest Regressor (Scikit-learn).

3. PROPOSED DATASET FIELDS

Potential input columns: - Quantity - Unit_Price - Discount_Percent -
Category (after categorical encoding) - Region (after categorical
encoding) - Sales_Channel (after categorical encoding)

Potential prediction target: - Profit (or Revenue; select one target for
each model)

The dataset must be checked for missing values, invalid records,
duplicates, and data leakage before training. Identifiers such as
Order_ID should not be used as predictive features.

4. PROPOSED WORKFLOW

1.  Load the sales Excel/CSV dataset using Pandas.

2.  Validate required columns and inspect data types.

3.  Clean missing, duplicate, and invalid records.

4.  Separate input features from the selected target.

5.  Encode categorical columns.

6.  Split data into training and test sets.

7.  Train a Random Forest Regressor on the training set.

8.  Evaluate predictions on the held-out test set.

9.  Record metrics such as MAE, RMSE, and R-squared.

10. Save the fitted pipeline only after successful training.

11. Integrate prediction functionality into the PulseBI interface.

12. MODEL EVALUATION

    Mean Absolute Error (MAE): Average absolute difference between
    actual and predicted values.

Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE): Square root of the average squared
prediction error.

R-squared (R²): Measures how much target variation is explained by the
model. A negative R² is possible and indicates poor performance.

No metric values are included here because no training run has been
performed as part of this documentation.

6. PROPOSED FILE STRUCTURE

model/ training_documentation.txt train_model.py (to be implemented)
trained_model.pkl (created only after successful training)

7. IMPORTANT IMPLEMENTATION NOTES

-   A .pkl file should contain a real fitted model/pipeline, not an
    empty or manually created placeholder.
-   Keep the training dataset separate from the test dataset.
-   Do not claim training or accuracy results until the script has
    actually run.
-   The model should be retrained when the dataset or feature
    definitions change.
-   Local model training does not require a paid external AI API.
-   If the model is later exposed through an API, protect credentials
    and handle unavailable services gracefully.

8. CURRENT STATUS

Documentation prepared. Model training: Not yet performed. Model
evaluation: Not yet performed. Application integration: Not yet
performed.

This status should be updated only after the corresponding work is
completed.
